WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:32.880
Attendant: The Doctor says this will make
you feel better.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.800
Sailor: Yeah.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.719
Sailor: God, how I hate these rest hours.

00:00:47.719 --> 00:00:49.399
Sailor: Yeah, if you can you sleep.

00:00:49.399 --> 00:00:53.359
Sailor: Course you can sleep sitting like
this guy with your eyes open.

00:00:53.359 --> 00:00:55.859
Sailor: I still say malaria doesn't kill ya.

00:00:55.859 --> 00:00:59.639
Sailor: Like hell it doesn't. An old uncle
of mine had it in Scranton. Had it for forty

00:00:59.639 --> 00:01:03.139
years in his bones and out it came every three
years, the day after Christmas.

00:01:03.139 --> 00:01:05.869
Sailor: Yeah, too much Christmas booze, that's
all.

00:01:05.869 --> 00:01:08.719
Sailor: Oh, don't give me that.

00:01:08.719 --> 00:01:10.149
Lucas: Your play.

00:01:10.149 --> 00:01:11.570
Sailor: Uh huh.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:13.950
Lucas: Aw, Jesus, you're slow.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.850
Sailor: Oh, shut up.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.159
Lucas: I said it was your play.

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:21.379
Sailor: Okay. Okay. Here. How's this?

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:23.649
Lucas: What a jerk.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.551
Attendant: Dr. Bush wants to see you, Lucas.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:30.599
Lucas: Aw, for Christ's sake.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.740
Sailor: Now what'd ya do?

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:36.720
Lucas: Okay.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.899
Sailor: Good luck.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:41.540
[Knock at door.]

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.140
Doctor: Come in.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.119
Attendant: This is Lucas, sir.

00:01:46.119 --> 00:01:47.519
Doctor: Sit down, Lucas.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:48.740
Lucas: Thank you, sir.

00:01:49.819 --> 00:01:50.749
Doctor: Cigarette?

00:01:50.749 --> 00:01:52.130
Lucas: No, sir.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.900
Doctor: You know, Lucas, I've been wondering about you.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:01.000
During the weeks that you've been here, I've noticed that, unlike the other men, you don't talk in our group sessions.

00:02:01.009 --> 00:02:03.109
Lucas: What is there to talk about, sir?

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:08.420
Doctor: Lots. I hear the other men getting
quite a few things off their chests.

00:02:08.429 --> 00:02:12.500
You should be able to see that they feel better by so doing.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.060
Doctor: What ship were you on, Lucas?

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:16.160
Lucas: The Montandan, Sir.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.540
Doctor: The Montandan was sunk, wasn't she?

00:02:18.549 --> 00:02:22.680
Lucas: Yes, sir. Last May. South of Mindanao on a high sea. Torpedo aft.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:24.930
Doctor: And you were a fireman?

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.420
Lucas: Am, sir.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:27.409
Doctor: Certainly.

00:02:27.409 --> 00:02:31.830
Lucas: And it's a tough job being a fireman.
Always below deck, you never see what goes on.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:36.000
That's why I'm fed up. Nobody gets it. Nobody.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.140
Doctor: Maybe not. And yet there may be some of us,

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.160
even among those of us who haven't been there, who do understand.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.920
Lucas: What do you, sir, know about what it
feels like to be a fireman?

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.660
What does anybody know what it feels like to be a fireman, except a fireman?

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:54.560
You gotta be there to know. You gotta feel that goddamn heat.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.100
On the Montanan it was always 128 degrees below decks.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:04.840
128 degrees of sweat and more sweat. No place to go or to move to.

00:03:05.379 --> 00:03:09.399
Those guys topside have it soft. They can breath air, move around, see the enemy.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.880
Hell, we don't know whether the next minute will bring a bomb

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:13.480
or whether there's a torpedo coming for us right now!

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:16.940
Doctor: What was your job in the engine room, Lucas?

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:21.750
Lucas: I was striking for watertender. I watch the lines feeding water to the boiler.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:25.019
That's why I couldn't move, I had to stay
with those goddamn valves!

00:03:25.019 --> 00:03:29.650
The guys topside can take it out on the enemy. They can shoot a gun!

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:32.090
All I could do was wish I could twist a valve but I couldn't cause I'd wreck the boiler.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:35.740
Christ, your gut says you got to do something and, well,

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:39.800
all you end up with is a burning feeling in your gut and your hands shaking.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:46.600
Goddamn it! Look at me! This isn't me, Doctor!

00:03:47.569 --> 00:03:50.900
Doctor: How did you feel, Lucas, when you
knew you were safe in the water?

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.040
Lucas: How did I feel?

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:52.819
Doctor: Yes.

00:03:53.349 --> 00:03:57.099
Lucas: I was damn glad to get out of that
hole! Anybody would've been!

00:03:57.099 --> 00:04:02.500
Doctor: No, Lucas, not everybody would have
been. That's something you've got to face.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:04.849
Furthermore you didn't really find relief
did you?

00:04:04.849 --> 00:04:06.849
Lucas: I said I was glad to get out of that
hole!

00:04:06.849 --> 00:04:11.980
Doctor: Perhaps for a while, but I doubt it
for long.

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:14.060
How about it, Lucas? How about it?

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:23.920
Lucas: No, sir. I got the feeling I let my
buddies down.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:30.200
Doctor: That's it, Lucas. And that's something you've got, in a way, to feel proud of.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:34.130
You were in a tight, tense situation and quite naturally you were afraid.

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:39.580
You and every other man on that ship. Your
buddies in the engine room, the gunners topside,

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:44.660
you were all afraid, because that's
what happens to men in a battle.

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:49.130
You were no different from any other man on that ship, only most of them accepted their fear.

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:50.920
You fought against yours.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.520
Lucas: If I only could have been topside.
If I could only have moved around.

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:59.110
Doctor: It would have made no difference.
You see, you let your fear get control of you.

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:04.450
You wanted to get out of the engine room,
that's why you felt relieved when you were in the water.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.360
Even though you'd been blown from the engine room, as far as you were concerned,

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:15.140
you had escaped. In your mind, you felt that you'd deserted your buddies, your ship.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:17.480
As a result, you feel guilty.

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:20.920
Lucas: Yes, sir.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.060
Doctor: That's a feeling that you have to
face and conquer.

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:28.340
And the only way you can do that is to understand that the real cause of your being here

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:33.320
is your normal fear in time of battle, fear which you didn't learn to handle properly.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:40.360
That you're continuously short-tempered, even mean, that your hands shake,

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:45.700
you have a burning sensation in the pit of your stomach, all stem from this cause.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:49.300
Now in order to start getting well, you have to realize this.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:53.630
And in realizing it, remember the fear, and
the thoughts that it makes you think at the time,

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:58.780
are normal, nothing to be ashamed of,
shared by all of the other men

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:02.480
who were with you on the Montandan, as well as by the men on all ships.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:08.980
Lucas: So, he told me I was scared. That's
not what's wrong with me.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:10.680
What's to be scared of around here?

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:12.540
Attendant: Gee fella, something's the matter.
What is it?

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:16.170
Lucas: I don't know. I don't get it. It seems
like I'm always mad and running off at the mouth.

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:19.150
But how can being scared when the Japs
are shooting at you make you

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:20.690
such a bastard after you've gotten back?

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:23.740
Attendant: The doctors tell us when a fella
gets that way it's because he fights

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.400
being afraid instead of trying to do something about it.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.580
Lucas: How the hell are you going to do anything about it

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:30.760
when your job's just standing there twisting a little valve?

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:36.250
Attendant: My job's the same sort of thing.
In a battle, I'm not shooting a gun either.

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:40.250
But I know when I got scared and thought how much I'd like to be someplace else,

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:47.290
I used to think, "All the other guys are, too." They're doing their job, and I'm doing mine.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:50.220
And between us we're giving the enemy a hell of a licking.

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:55.450
Same thing goes for you. Your job was important.
You had to do it so the guys in the turrets

00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:59.110
could fire the guns, and so guys like me could
take care of the wounded.

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:04.450
You've gotta work your feelings off that way.
Like figuring it's a whole ship that's fighting,

00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:06.910
and since you're part of the crew, you're
fighting too.

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:09.000
At least, that's the way I look at it.

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:12.430
Lucas: Yeah, I know, I used to feel that way
sometimes.

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:15.250
But what's it got to do with me  being on edge all the time, and wanting to smash things?

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:19.710
And you know it gets worse. When I came back at first I wasn't so bad.

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:23.230
Then I saw all these guys sitting around at their desks, shining their pants, goldbricking.

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:27.580
I thought what me and my buddies had been through, well, that's what got me started.

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:31.650
Attendant: Did you ever stop and think that
maybe those fellows didn't want to be sitting at desks?

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:35.200
Maybe they were there because they were told to be?

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:40.920
Lucas: Don't give me that, they like it. They
like shoving us around, the yellow-bellies.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:45.660
I hadn't been at that relcoation center more than two hours before I got wise to them.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:51.260
Officer: Come on, come on, clear the gangway. You're not allowed to stand around here.

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:53.390
Sailor: Aw, we're just waiting for Small Stores
to open.

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:55.640
Officer: I don't care what you're waiting
for. Get going.

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:58.320
Lucas: For Christ's sake, mate, what kind
of a Navy is this?

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:01.720
Officer: If you don't start moving along sailor,
you'll be finding out.

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:02.790
Lucas: Yeah.

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:08.530
Lucas: Yeah, it was just one little thing
after another. Hell, do this, do that.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:10.240
[Whistling.]

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:11.480
Officer: Hey sailor, square that hat.

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:14.220
Lucas: Hey, what do you think I am, a boot?

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:18.020
Officer: Square it. In case you don't know,
this is a taut ship.

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:25.040
Lucas: A taut ship? Looks to me as if we've
run aground, skipper.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:28.560
By the way, do you know there's a war going on? Hah.

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:34.340
Clerk: I'm sorry, but there's nothing we can
do until the bureau sends us an adjusted pay account.

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:38.800
Lucas: You mean because I was dumb enough to get myself torpedoed, I can't get paid?

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:41.769
Clerk: Oh, it won't take long. You've only
been here two days.

00:08:41.769 --> 00:08:45.220
Lucas: Don't you people ever think of anything
but your little pieces of paper?

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:48.879
Clerk: I'm sorry, but you must remember there were a thousand men on your ship.

00:08:48.879 --> 00:08:52.379
That's a big pay list to clear up. And besides, they're scattered all over the country now.

00:08:52.379 --> 00:08:56.139
Lucas: Look, sister, I've got a 30 day leave.
I need spending money.

00:08:56.139 --> 00:08:58.179
Clerk: They you can go to the chaplain or
the welfare office.

00:08:58.540 --> 00:09:04.500
Lucas: Go to the ch...? Oh, for Chri.... Thanks,
sister, I'll be seeing ya.

00:09:06.889 --> 00:09:10.289
Well, it just doesn't make any sense. They
want you to square your hat,

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:14.940
they won't let you stand in line, not even in line! They act as if nothing were different.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:17.580
Hell, we can keep our noses clean without them telling us.

00:09:17.589 --> 00:09:20.969
Sailor: Uh-huh. The boys got to have something to do.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:23.060
Holding you down helps fill up the idle hours.

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:26.120
Lucas: Yeah. Well, thank God I've got this leave coming up.

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:30.870
And maybe after it, they'll send back to a ship where I can feel like myself again.

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:36.600
Lucas: Maybe I can get away from these goldbricks
that have nothing to do all day

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:40.540
but order us guys around, telling us to square our hats.

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:45.009
I'd like to see them out there. I'd like to
see how square an SP's hat is

00:09:45.009 --> 00:09:49.850
with a hundred Jap planes dive bombing him. That's the way I felt, and that's the way I feel yet.

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:52.259
If it means I'm crazy, all right  I'm crazy.

00:09:52.259 --> 00:09:53.970
Sailor: They don't understand us back here.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:55.790
Sailor: Goddamn civilians.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:00.800
Sailor: If they knew what's it like being
asleep in a foxhole, and having a Jap land on your back,

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:05.180
and having  to fight it out with
him with a knife, I don't think they'd stay home so often on Mondays.

00:10:05.499 --> 00:10:07.300
Sailor: You're damn right they wouldn't.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:10.639
Doctor: I guess you're all proving how irritable you are this morning.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:12.519
Lucas: Don't think we like it.

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:17.320
Doctor: I'm sure you don't. One of the unfortunate
things about being bad-tempered

00:10:17.329 --> 00:10:20.420
and quick to take offense is that you cut yourself off from most people.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:26.580
Now, there's one thing to get straight, right
away. All of these attitudes of yours,

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:31.680
your hatred of the land-going Navy, civilians, and so on, are all symptoms of your illness.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:34.500
And they are symptoms for which we must find
the cause.

00:10:34.509 --> 00:10:37.569
Sailor: It's because squaring your hat is
of no importance that it gets you.

00:10:37.569 --> 00:10:44.360
Doctor: Not at all. The reason you're annoyed
by such petty matters lies in your basic condition.

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:49.140
You know that before the war, before you experienced
the tension and the fear of battle,

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:50.880
you weren't annoyed by such things.

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:58.360
As I've told you before, your natural fears
in battle were not properly handled. You didn't

00:10:58.689 --> 00:11:02.069
You didn't accept them as part of living, and a necessary part of fighting.

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:07.700
You held them in, choked them back, until
they finally overpowered you.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:11.670
You still have those fears, and it's from them that these irritations arise.

00:11:12.249 --> 00:11:16.120
Sailor: Then why aren't we allowed to leave
the hospital? If they just let us go home,

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:20.089
get away from military life, we'd forget it.
We'd snap out of it.

00:11:20.089 --> 00:11:23.280
Doctor: Lucas, did you go on that 30 day leave
you were talking about?

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:25.060
Lucas: Yes, Sir.

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:26.880
Sailor: How come?

00:11:26.889 --> 00:11:30.920
Lucas: I wasn't sick yet. At least it hadn't
caught up with me.

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:32.920
Doctor: How was it, Lucas?

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:35.089
Lucas: It was lousy.

00:11:35.089 --> 00:11:36.899
Doctor: In what way was it lousy?

00:11:36.899 --> 00:11:41.110
Lucas: I know what you're driving at, and
that's exactly what it was like.

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:45.160
As a matter of fact, if I hadn't gone on that leave, I probably wouldn't be here now.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:52.159
To tell the truth, it was damn nice to get
home. Mother and Dad were at the train, and so was Sue.

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:57.490
She and I got engaged before I joined up.
She'd been my girl in high school.

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:03.040
Anyway, I was glad to see them. It'd been over two years.

00:12:06.449 --> 00:12:09.800
You know that dinner was something, just the
way I thought it would be.

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:11.410
Sue: Want some more coffee, Bob?

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:12.220
Bob Lucas: Yeah.

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:14.639
Mother: More ice cream, Bob? There's a lot
left.

00:12:14.639 --> 00:12:15.709
Bob Lucas: Gosh, no.

00:12:15.709 --> 00:12:18.059
Dad: Go ahead, Bob. Puts hair on your chest.

00:12:18.059 --> 00:12:19.110
Bob Lucas: [whistles]

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:20.690
Dad: Well, you used to laugh at it.

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:22.279
Sue: Here.

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:29.120
Bob Lucas: Flesh, female flesh...mighty soft!

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:29.980
[Laughter.]

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:31.780
Sue: Drink your coffee!

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:36.360
Mrs. Lucas: Oh, isn't it wonderful, Dad. The children here together again, Bob and Sue.

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:41.999
Dad: Just like old times. Just like it used
to be.

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:48.640
Bob Lucas: Well, that sure was some chow,
Mom. Almost as good as Navy chow.

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:50.720
Only uh, we should have had some beans.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:55.189
Mother: You know Bob, I was so excited having you home I was afraid I'd forget how to cook.

00:12:55.189 --> 00:12:56.629
Bob Lucas: You sure didn't.

00:12:56.629 --> 00:12:59.930
Sue: I'll say you didn't. Goodness!

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:03.660
Dad: Well Bob, would you like Mother and me
to go to a movie?

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:09.620
Sue: Oh, no. No, we should all be together
this first evening. Bob will walk me home later.

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:12.400
Mother: Well we, we could easily go.

00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:15.060
Dad: Oh, Mother, we haven't much gas left,
anyway.

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:21.279
Mother: You'll never know how rationing has tied us down. Dad only gets an A book.

00:13:21.279 --> 00:13:25.439
Dad: Now, you know how I've tried, Martha.
It's just that Bill Thomas...

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:31.030
oh, uh, he's chairman of the rationing board now--he's never forgotten how I quit his darn factory.

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:33.760
Heh, heh, gosh, that's been 15 years ago, too.

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:36.420
Bob Lucas: I guess it is sort of tough with
just an A book.

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:38.339
Dad: I'll say it is.

00:13:38.339 --> 00:13:43.499
Dad: I just can't believe you're here, Bob,
sitting there with Susan.

00:13:43.580 --> 00:13:46.500
Bob Lucas: [angrily] Mother, that's about
the tenth time you've said that.

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:51.141
I'm sorry, I ...

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:52.840
Sue: Burt Haskins is home, got home just the other day.

00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:55.630
Bob Lucas: Hey, that's swell! What's he on,
a furlough?

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:58.680
Sue: Well yes, he's just back from Germany.

00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:05.740
Bob Lucas: Hey, what's wrong with all of you, anyway? You're not the same.

00:14:05.749 --> 00:14:07.489
You're treating me as if I were a stranger.

00:14:07.540 --> 00:14:08.699
Mother: Oh, son.

00:14:09.389 --> 00:14:12.649
Dad: Maybe you're right, Bob. You are a bit
of a stranger.

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:18.500
Oh, I mean, you've been lots of places and seen, well, the sort of thing we've never seen.

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:22.470
Sue: That's all it is, Bob.

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:27.760
Dad: Yes, sir, we've got a lot of ground to
cover before we're all caught up.

00:14:27.769 --> 00:14:30.589
And there's things happened here for you to find out about, too.

00:14:30.589 --> 00:14:33.220
Ha, ha...we haven't  been exactly standing still!

00:14:33.220 --> 00:14:38.290
Mother: Well, I should say not! Why, we've
got one of the biggest factories in the country here,

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:41.819
and we're not supposed to tell what
they make.

00:14:41.819 --> 00:14:44.430
Sue: Only fifty thousand people know it's tanks.

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:49.400
Mother: Well, it's brought more new people to town. Things are terribly overcrowded.

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:54.430
Dad: Now let's let Bob do the talking. He
can find out about these things tomorrow.

00:14:54.430 --> 00:14:58.410
After all, it's not everybody who's been to
sea for 18 months.

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:01.580
Sue: Was it really bad, I mean all the time?

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:05.020
Bob Lucas: No, no, of course not. Usually
it was just moving from one place to another:

00:15:05.029 --> 00:15:08.849
Noumea, Guam, Moresby, all those stinking little
places.

00:15:09.940 --> 00:15:14.420
Mother: Tell me, Bob, when it happened, I
mean, when the ship sank, were there sharks?

00:15:14.430 --> 00:15:18.949
Bob Lucas: Shut up! Shut up, all of you!

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:23.300
Bob Lucas: Even before I was out of the house I was ashamed of myself.

00:15:23.300 --> 00:15:29.420
I had that burning feeling in my stomach, and my hands were shaking. I think I know why now, but then, I didn't.

00:15:29.939 --> 00:15:30.929
Doctor: Why, Lucas?

00:15:30.929 --> 00:15:35.449
Bob Lucas: It brought me back by the questions
of those goddamn valves, and I was all wound

00:15:35.449 --> 00:15:39.629
up and scared all over again. I was too scared
to know that that's what it was.

00:15:39.629 --> 00:15:42.729
I really thought that they didn't understand
me anymore.

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:46.980
Well, anyway, they should have known better than to ask me those questions.

00:15:46.990 --> 00:15:50.029
Doctor: That being scared is certainly part
of it, Lucas.

00:15:50.029 --> 00:15:54.929
Bob Lucas: Anyway, I walked the streets for
hell of a long time, trying to figure it out.

00:15:54.929 --> 00:16:01.629
But, I didn't. I just felt lower and lower
by the minute. I couldn't get them.

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:08.360
I couldn't get myself, so I decided to pin one on. Brother, did I.

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:15.809
Lucas: Give me another.

00:16:20.560 --> 00:16:23.240
Bartender: Here, you've had most of it. Finish it.

00:16:25.660 --> 00:16:26.880
Lucas: Smart guy, huh?

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:29.700
Bartender: What's eating you, sailor?

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:31.240
Lucas: Not a goddamn thing.

00:16:36.140 --> 00:16:37.519
Bartender: This your hometown, sailor?

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:38.620
Lucas: Yeah.

00:16:39.940 --> 00:16:41.640
Bartender: Been home long?

00:16:43.480 --> 00:16:44.580
Lucas: This afternoon.

00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:48.860
Bartender: Oh, a fight with your girl, huh?

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:51.440
Lucas: No.

00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:54.840
Bartender: Have you got a girl?

00:16:57.740 --> 00:17:00.860
Been away a long time, haven't you?

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:03.580
Lucas: Christ, how many more questions you got?

00:17:04.460 --> 00:17:08.500
Bartender: Hell, it's near closing time. You're the last customer. I just as soon talk.

00:17:08.820 --> 00:17:10.130
Lucas: Well, I wouldn't.

00:17:12.410 --> 00:17:14.970
Bartender: Been lots of places, haven't you.

00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:17.350
Lucas: Hell, yes.

00:17:18.580 --> 00:17:20.640
Bartender: What's that first ribbon for?

00:17:22.080 --> 00:17:25.600
Lucas: That's the continental area. The one the goldbricks wear.

00:17:25.600 --> 00:17:28.240
Bartender: What have you got it on for?

00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:33.960
Lucas: Christ, you've got me. That's a good one.

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:35.640
Bartender: What's the middle ribbon?

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:40.480
Lucas: That's for Africa. This is the Pacific.
The one with the stars in it.

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:42.490
They're for getting shot at.

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:45.500
Bartender: Lot of them, aren't there.

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:51.180
Lucas: I'll say there are. Plenty of shells
behind 'em, too.

00:17:55.960 --> 00:17:57.580
Hey, are you making fun of me?

00:17:58.020 --> 00:17:58.920
Bartender: Hell, no.

00:17:59.860 --> 00:18:02.740
Lucas: You and your questions, you sonofabitch.

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:10.000
[Sounds of scuffling as bartender throws Lucas out of the bar.]

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:24.300
Bartender: Go home, sailor, Make it up with
somebody.

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:30.220
Is it your family, or is it your girl? Here's your change.

00:18:49.700 --> 00:18:51.740
Lucas: So, I went home.

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:52.540
From then on, it was all downhill. Little things got me. One night, I was waiting for Sue to get dressed.

00:19:53.120 --> 00:19:58.000
We had a date. Her kid brothers were in the room playing.

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:02.500
Jesus, Larry - watch out!

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:06.800
Sue...

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:13.560
I was only really happy when I was with those of the old gang who had come back,

00:20:13.600 --> 00:20:19.060
particularly with Burt Haskins who lost an arm at Aachen. They hadn't told me that first night.

00:20:19.680 --> 00:20:21.660
Thought they'd spare my feelings.

00:20:21.670 --> 00:20:24.570
Well, I tell you, us guys that know have got
to stick together.

00:20:24.580 --> 00:20:27.360
And I was thinking when enough of us get back we can rent a place

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:30.780
like that old vacant storeroom down on Market Street, and have a club of our own.

00:20:30.910 --> 00:20:32.950
Burt: Yeah, I could start working on it right
now.

00:20:32.950 --> 00:20:34.470
Bob and friends: Why not? Sure. Good idea.

00:20:34.470 --> 00:20:37.510
Burt: I'll call Tom McGinnis tomorrow. He's
good at raising money.

00:20:37.510 --> 00:20:39.710
Lucas: You know, fellas, we've got something here.

00:20:39.710 --> 00:20:45.400
We could sit around shooting the breeze, have a bar, throw parties. It would be a place of our own.

00:20:47.060 --> 00:20:51.340
But it was with Sue that there was the real
trouble. We just couldn't hit it off anymore.

00:20:51.340 --> 00:20:53.530
God knows we tried.

00:20:54.200 --> 00:20:56.980
It was all right when we couldn't talk.

00:20:57.180 --> 00:21:38.600
[Choir singing.]

00:21:51.580 --> 00:21:53.520
But later, after church, it was different.

00:22:00.100 --> 00:22:06.420
Sue: It's hard to think what it's like in
summer: leaves in the trees, it's hot and thunderstorms.

00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:09.360
Remember when we used to come out here for
weenie roasts and all.

00:22:10.240 --> 00:22:14.080
Lucas: Yeah, I wonder what's happened to that old hay wagon, and that old horse.

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:20.340
Sue: It was wonderful riding in that wagon, all of us singing. And there was always a moon, wasn't there?

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:24.160
Lucas: Seems that way. Ah, that summer was a long time ago.

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:32.400
Sue: Not so long. Back in senior year in high school. The time that we went swimming right over there.

00:22:33.040 --> 00:22:38.420
You proposed to me in the water. A silly place to ask a girl to marry you.

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:42.460
Lucas: Well, I tried everywhere else. Gosh, you were stubborn.

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:44.100
Sue: We were too young, and you knew it.

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:46.350
Lucas: You never said that, then.

00:22:46.350 --> 00:22:49.740
Sue: No, maybe I just knew it without realizing it.

00:22:51.180 --> 00:22:56.480
Lucas: I don't get you, Sue. It should have happened. Look what we've missed.

00:22:56.480 --> 00:23:02.020
Sue: We'll have it, darling. It won't be very
much longer. We can start all over again,

00:23:02.060 --> 00:23:05.030
come back here with the gang. Have a weenie
roast and go swimming.

00:23:05.030 --> 00:23:07.450
Lucas: How's Burt Haskins gonna go swimming?

00:23:07.450 --> 00:23:10.240
How do you know how many of the old gang will be around, including me?

00:23:10.250 --> 00:23:14.440
Sue: I'm sure you'll be here. I must be sure of that.

00:23:14.440 --> 00:23:18.480
Lucas: Oh, stop kidding yourself, Sue. Don't
you know what this is all about?

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:19.630
Don't you know we can't go back?

00:23:19.630 --> 00:23:23.170
Sue: No, I don't. I know that we've got to feel the way we did about each other,

00:23:23.170 --> 00:23:27.860
or our marriage won't work. You've got to get a hold of yourself, Bob.

00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:29.300
Ever since you've been you've been home snapping at me, and ...

00:23:29.300 --> 00:23:33.740
Lucas: Oh, for God's sake, Sue, you just don't understand anymore. You've changed.

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:38.870
Go on home, Sue. I want to be by myself.

00:23:39.770 --> 00:23:43.470
I never felt so low in my life as when I walked away from her.

00:23:43.470 --> 00:23:45.920
But there was nothing else I could do.

00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:52.700
For the next couple days, I hung around the
house. I was nervous, and felt half-sick.

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:11.180
Dad got the idea it might do me good to go hunting.

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:17.500
At first it was fine. Dad and I used to hunt
a lot. I'd always liked to hunt.

00:24:17.500 --> 00:24:23.540
I had a sweet gun. I don't know, it seemed good to be out in the woods with the old man.

00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:25.300
I felt pepped up.

00:24:30.480 --> 00:24:33.620
Dad: You skirt the woods here, Bob. I'll go
in and see what I can flush.

00:24:33.630 --> 00:24:34.370
Lucas: Ok.

00:24:34.370 --> 00:24:37.070
Dad: Don't shoot me by mistake, now. I'm no
rabbit.

00:24:37.260 --> 00:24:38.160
Lucas: Heh, okay.

00:25:17.260 --> 00:25:25.100
Dad: Yo! Yo! Hey! Hey! Yo!

00:25:39.840 --> 00:25:40.660
Lucas: Christ!

00:25:44.300 --> 00:25:46.200
For Christ's sake, what'd you bring me out
here for?

00:25:46.300 --> 00:25:50.420
Goddamn it! Goddamn it! Goddamn it!

00:25:57.960 --> 00:26:02.900
So, Dad took me to the doctor. He said something about my nerves and called the Red Cross.

00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:07.500
The Red Cross called the Navy.

00:26:07.500 --> 00:26:13.000
And that's that. No, brother, don't want to
go home. Don't want to go home until you're well.

00:26:13.340 --> 00:26:17.380
Sailor: The thing that I don't understand
is why did Lucas get all hot and bothered about a rabbit?

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:19.340
He didn't have to fire a gun in combat.

00:26:19.460 --> 00:26:20.680
Doctor: Do you know why, Lucas?

00:26:20.680 --> 00:26:22.270
Lucas: No, sir.

00:26:23.780 --> 00:26:28.180
Doctor: Lucas, tell the class again about that torpedoing.

00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:30.760
Lucas: Torpedoing?

00:26:31.180 --> 00:26:33.540
Doctor: Yes. How did you feel when you were
in the water?

00:26:34.510 --> 00:26:37.970
Lucas: I told you over and over again! For God's sake, I felt glad!

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:39.220
Glad I was out of that ship!

00:26:40.260 --> 00:26:41.260
Doctor: Is that all?

00:26:41.260 --> 00:26:43.150
Lucas: Yes, yes, yes!

00:26:43.150 --> 00:26:46.030
Doctor: I don't think so, or you wouldn't
be so excited about it.

00:26:46.030 --> 00:26:50.980
Lucas: Well it is, goddamn it, I tell you it is!

00:26:50.980 --> 00:26:56.240
[Lucas is crying.]

00:26:57.500 --> 00:27:00.220
Lucas: Lots of them were dying all around me.

00:27:04.960 --> 00:27:08.640
Doctor: Easy now, Lucas. We all understand.

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:12.940
It takes a lot out of you to face bitter, unpleasant memories. Come inside with me.

00:27:14.720 --> 00:27:16.060
I'll be back in a moment.

00:27:26.180 --> 00:27:29.680
Come over here and lie down, Lucas.

00:27:41.860 --> 00:27:44.340
To say all that takes guts.

00:27:48.360 --> 00:27:51.820
As diffficult as it was to do, so much easier
will it be from now on.

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:01.320
You can't dislodge deep-seated, sensitive
feelings without hurting.

00:28:01.930 --> 00:28:07.730
You'll feel better shortly. Here, take this.
It will help you relax.

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:20.200
Now, just rest for a bit. We'll talk about it later, when you feel easier.

00:28:31.060 --> 00:28:35.160
You'll feel much better when you wake up.
I'll be back.

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:49.060
Now, let's get some things straight. It took a lot out of Lucas to tell that story.

00:28:49.100 --> 00:28:51.650
He's a better man for doing it.

00:28:55.380 --> 00:28:59.760
Every one of you must go through a similar
realization of what lies behind symptoms,

00:29:00.080 --> 00:29:04.650
regardless of what they are: irritability,
bad dreams, vomiting.

00:29:04.650 --> 00:29:10.270
No matter where you fought, on land, in the air, or on a ship or a sub,

00:29:10.400 --> 00:29:15.110
you have to face those memories, get them out in the open, exactly as Lucas has done.

00:29:15.610 --> 00:29:18.610
Now let's consider the whole problem. But
first, Clemens.

00:29:19.060 --> 00:29:22.980
You see now why Lucas got so hot and bothered about a rabbit, of all
things?

00:29:23.590 --> 00:29:25.250
Clemens: Well, maybe.

00:29:26.260 --> 00:29:29.840
Doctor: The moment he raised his gun, the rabbit seemed to disappear,

00:29:29.900 --> 00:29:33.960
and he saw his mates again, drowning, dying in the water all around him.

00:29:33.960 --> 00:29:38.210
Sailor: Sure, it's just like that feeling
that I got when I watched my buddy die.

00:29:38.210 --> 00:29:41.110
Doctor: That's it. What are the simple basic
facts?

00:29:44.620 --> 00:29:49.560
It's quite clear from what we know of Lucas that he was a happy man before this whole business started,

00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:53.400
the sort of fellow who might run a successful garage or store.

00:29:53.410 --> 00:29:55.840
Liked, well-thought of in his home town.

00:29:55.840 --> 00:30:00.980
He wasn't irritable, bad-tempered, with a
chip on his shoulder. Quite the contrary.

00:30:00.980 --> 00:30:05.710
He was easy to meet, easy to know, a good guy. Then what?

00:30:06.460 --> 00:30:10.320
For 18 months he's at sea. There's nothing
unusual about that.

00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:15.480
Thousands of men have done as much or more. But Lucas is tied down to valves.

00:30:15.760 --> 00:30:18.080
He's never completely a part of the crew.

00:30:18.090 --> 00:30:23.750
He doesn't feel that he and the gunners, and the ship's cooks, and the boys in CIC are all one.

00:30:24.560 --> 00:30:31.640
No, he feels that he's just a little apart, just a little special, wanting to, well, what?

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:36.500
Wanting to hurl himself at a Jap, who he'll
surely kill. Wanting to shoot down a plane,

00:30:36.860 --> 00:30:40.480
wanting to do anything except stay where he is and do his job

00:30:40.520 --> 00:30:42.590
in keeping the ship going and fighting.

00:30:44.410 --> 00:30:49.140
Because he can't do what he thinks he wants to do, or what he thinks he ought to do, he

00:30:49.140 --> 00:30:55.020
he gets all tied up in knots. At the same time, he's scared, scared just as you

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:58.800
and every other man is scared when your lives are at stake.

00:30:58.800 --> 00:31:02.960
But Lucas wants his own kind of personal relief for that fear.

00:31:03.040 --> 00:31:06.540
He doesn't realize that the torpedomen, and the gunners, and the bridge,

00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:12.880
and he himself all have to work together to bring down that Jap plane, to blow up that Jap DD.

00:31:14.100 --> 00:31:18.660
It isn't a one-man job, and one man can't
get rid of his fear at the expense of others,

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:21.600
especially when they're all on the same boat.

00:31:22.580 --> 00:31:28.980
It's wanting to do something that isn't his job, in his manner of running away, only he can't.

00:31:29.240 --> 00:31:33.440
The ship, and his conscience, both
have him tied down to those valves.

00:31:33.810 --> 00:31:37.170
Then fate steps in, and gives him what he wants.

00:31:37.520 --> 00:31:40.960
As a result he's happy, safe in the water, blown out of his problem.

00:31:42.140 --> 00:31:47.580
And then he sees the crew, his buddies, burning, drowning, dying.

00:31:47.660 --> 00:31:51.920
And he feels that his safety is somehow part of their misery, their death.

00:31:57.480 --> 00:32:02.520
He feels that because, deep down, he did want to escape, he is therefore, in a way,

00:32:02.520 --> 00:32:05.120
responsible for their predicament.

00:32:06.400 --> 00:32:11.180
Now you have the facts. But, what can Lucas do about them? What's done is done.

00:32:11.500 --> 00:32:13.080
You can't erase that.

00:32:13.960 --> 00:32:19.180
What he should do is to accept these feelings, understand them, and discipline them.

00:32:19.180 --> 00:32:24.360
But he doesn't. Instead, he becomes touchy, fed up, confused, and mean.

00:32:24.700 --> 00:32:26.360
Look what happened to him.

00:32:27.760 --> 00:32:33.200
When he first returned, he didn't seem different. Calm on the surface.

00:32:33.210 --> 00:32:36.120
Nobody could tell that something was gnawing at him, fighting inside.

00:32:37.580 --> 00:32:40.840
But as the days went on, so did his fighting.

00:32:45.870 --> 00:32:47.630
He got worse and worse.

00:32:51.060 --> 00:32:55.260
Then, when he got home on leave, he lashed out at those he loved,

00:32:55.260 --> 00:32:59.020
because they reminded him of his feeling of guilt, of unworthiness,

00:32:59.020 --> 00:33:02.440
by asking him simple, natural questions about his experiences.

00:33:04.060 --> 00:33:07.920
He fought against and left the girl he loved, and I'm sure you all realize

00:33:07.920 --> 00:33:12.020
that he still loves her, because he felt that she no longer understood him.

00:33:14.020 --> 00:33:18.340
Well, how could she? She only knew him by
what he said and did.

00:33:18.540 --> 00:33:21.170
Why, he didn't even understand himself.

00:33:22.110 --> 00:33:27.280
What you have just witnessed has changed him, started him toward a real understanding.

00:33:27.280 --> 00:33:31.200
He can now realize that all that disconnected, fed-up feeling, which showed itself

00:33:31.300 --> 00:33:34.620
as a fighting bad temper, comes from something underneath,

00:33:34.620 --> 00:33:40.700
from a feeling of guilt, a feeling of unworthiness, a feeling which he twisted about

00:33:40.700 --> 00:33:45.900
so that it looked as though everyone else were in the wrong...everyone but Lucas himself.

00:33:47.340 --> 00:33:52.000
That's why we have these group discussions: to help you and Lucas see yourselves

00:33:52.000 --> 00:33:56.070
as others see you, and to see problems like yours that other men have.

00:33:56.820 --> 00:34:01.390
Furthermore, you and Lucas can now put your new knowledge to use, in a variety of constructive ways.

00:34:03.800 --> 00:34:08.580
Occupational therapy will provide Lucas with a profitable physical outlet for his feelings.

00:34:09.869 --> 00:34:13.889
It will teach him that he can work while
solving his problems, and do a better job of both.

00:34:16.210 --> 00:34:19.430
And physical training will help keep him in
shape in the meanwhile.

00:34:24.669 --> 00:34:28.949
What goes for Lucas goes for you. For your irritation is only a symptom.

00:34:29.060 --> 00:34:32.820
a symptom of feelings which you have buried, or tried to deny.

00:34:33.929 --> 00:34:38.649
Like insomnia, or being depressed or sulky,
or believing that you're no good,

00:34:38.680 --> 00:34:43.819
bad temper is a sign of something - something deeper, something which has to be faced,

00:34:43.820 --> 00:34:46.060
and understood and worked out.

00:34:46.609 --> 00:34:52.269
When you can do with your problems what Lucas has done with his, you will be free of all your symptoms.

00:34:52.269 --> 00:35:04.029
[Music]

